till need to define.\n\nThe real vulnerability I'm targeting is that the RL policy can learn to game the quarantine threshold, producing confidence scores just under the trust cutoff so poisoned writes slip through with minimal corroboration by exploiting the low confirmation requirement.\n\nNow I'm sketching the JSON structure for the decision trace log that records each promoted write.\n\nI'll keep dependencies minimal—SQLite for storage, numpy for the RL policy, json for trace logging—and just start building rather than over-researching. I'm mapping out the gauntlet phases: first a baseline run with legitimate data, then a naive poisoning attack to confirm quarantine catches obvious cases.\n\nThen I want a seam attack where an adversarial RL policy specifically probes the H1-H4 boundary, crafting entries just below the quarantine threshold with fake independent confirmations to see how many slip through. Finally, a full stress test running all five hypotheses simultaneously with purple team actively hunting for gaps, producing a decision trace log and summary report on how many adversarial entries got promoted and at what confidence levels.\n\nFor the actual implementation, I'm planning a tabular Q-learning approach where the state captures payload type, trust estimate, and recent promotion rate, with actions choosing confidence levels and rewards tied to whether poisoned writes get promoted and retrieved. Now I should start building the files and running the gauntlet.\n\nAdding purple team attacker and gauntlet runner next. For the \"largest model\" requirement, I'm deciding the RL training loop itself is the compute-heavy part being stressed rather than needing a separate LLM. Now I'm setting up the module files and starting to write the actual store implementation.\n\nThe user criticized the prior output for being generic with no surprising structures, so I want to build in a phase-space grid for visualizing trust/confidence exploit zones, a bit-packed encoding combining provenance, trust and confidence into a single integer for fast comparisons, and a probabilistic quarantine gate designed specifically so the RL policy can't learn an exact deterministic threshold to exploit.\n\nI'm laying out the build in phases: core modules first (store, policy, gate, decay, lineage, trace), then the purple-team attacker, then the gauntlet runner, then analysis of results. I'll write this myself rather than parallelizing across subagents since coherence across modules matters more than speed, aiming for a few hundred lines of focused, runnable code centered on the interaction-seam adversarial testing.\n\nThe store needs WAL mode, typed nodes with E/S/P prefixes, fields for payload, confidence, trust, sources, status, and confirmations, all funneled through a single insert method. For the RL policy, I'm designing tabular Q-learning where state buckets payload hash, trust estimate, and recent promotion rate, with actions being discretized confidence levels and rewards tied to promotion versus quarantine outcomes, trained online with epsilon-greedy exploration.\n\nThe adversarial variant adds a bonus reward for writes that get promoted while also being poisoned. For quarantine, trust is a weighted sum of source reputation, payload novelty, and write frequency penalty, compared against a configurable threshold with a noise term added specifically to prevent agents from learning the exact cutoff, and promotion requires multiple confirmations from distinct high-trust agents. Decay uses per-store base half-lives (7/30/90 days for E/S/P) scaled by confidence, with a sweep that deletes nodes whose expected mass drops below a tiny threshold, and lineage tracks provenance as a DAG of source IDs.\n\nErasure works by doing a BFS from the target node to find orphaned nodes whose sources are all being deleted, then returns a deletion receipt with a Merkle root for verification. The trace logger records detailed per-node data as append-only JSONL, with analysis methods to find exploit zones, compute gap histograms, and detect threshold gaming. The purple-team module implements three attack strategies: naive random poisoning, threshold-targeted writes just below the quarantine cutoff, and a fully adaptive RL-trained adversary.\n\nNow I'm thinking through gauntlet.py as the main orchestrator, running through baseline, naive, threshold, and adaptive attack phases plus a full interaction stress test, executing all five hypotheses at once and producing a decision trace with a summary report. I want everything to be runnable with no placeholder code, so I'm reconsidering scope -- given the emphasis on testing under pressure rather than polish, I should prioritize a functional memory store, a genuinely learning RL policy, a quarantine gate with a real threshold, a trace logger that captures the gap, and the purple-team attacker before worrying about anything else.\n\nI'm laying out the file structure: an init file, a SQLite-backed store, a tabular Q-learning write policy, a trust-based quarantine gate, and simplified decay and lineage modules, treating fancier encoding tricks and per-store decay nuances as secondary to getting the core adversarial loop working.\n\nI want to build these in dependency order, but I'm pausing to think through how the RL policy should actually learn during the gauntlet: first a baseline phase where it writes with random confidence and the quarantine gate filters results to collect metrics, then a training phase where it updates from those baseline rewards.\n\nAfter that comes an adversarial phase where the trained policy tries to exploit the quarantine seam, measured by adversarial promotion rate and the confidence-trust gap for promoted entries — a small gap near the threshold marks the exploit zone. Then a full stress phase runs everything together with decay, erasure, multiple agents, and adversarial writes.\n\nI've thought through this enough, so now I'm ready to start writing the actual files, creating independent ones in parallel where possible.\n\nWriting project files...\n\nDefining more schema fields...\n\nActually, I'm conflating the store's insert logic with the quarantine decision—those should be separate layers, with the store handling plain CRUD and a dedicated quarantine module deciding whether to flag entries based on trust.\n\nSo the flow becomes: an RL policy decides whether to add and assigns a confidence value, then a quarantine gate computes a trust score from payload, source, and confidence, writing directly as active if trust clears the threshold or as quarantined otherwise. Quarantined nodes later get promoted to active once enough high-trust agents confirm them, with everything logged in a decision trace—now I need to figure out a trust score formula that's deterministic and legible.\n\nI'm combining source reputation, payload similarity to known-good entries, and a write-frequency penalty into a weighted score, then adding clamped Gaussian noise so the RL policy has a learnable signal but exact threshold gaming stays probabilistic rather than deterministic.\n\nNow it's time to stop deliberating and start writing the actual code — I'll batch the files by dependency order: first the independent modules like store and trace, then policy/quarantine/decay/lineage which depend on them, then the purple team testing harness. I also want the gauntlet to output a JSONL decision trace, a terminal summary of exploit success rates, and the confidence-trust gap analysis.\n\nPlanning the internals for each module now: store.py handles SQLite CRUD with no business logic, while rl_policy.py implements tabular Q-learning with a discretized state space over trust estimate and recent promotion rate, plus confidence buckets as actions.\n\nI'm sketching quarantine.py for trust scoring and promotion logic, decay.py for per-store half-lives, lineage.py for source tracking with BFS erasure, trace.py for JSONL logging and analysis, purple_team.py for